#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494ef4f7-873c-5d87-85e5-89094c6cc376" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "7a6fbfc8-cd6d-57fe-bcab-ab68339683bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-155" :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-12$ | $-7$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $15$ | $21$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x) - f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=16.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(16) & ≈\\frac{f(16)-f(16-3)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(16)-f(13)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{21-15}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091908 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "cdf8854d-14ea-5564-81cc-4e5098f1acc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-156" :question/problem "A race car's position along a straight track, measured in meters from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ | $1.8$ | $2.0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $1.3$ | $2.7$ | $2.9$ | $3.5$ | $5.0$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ when $t=1.6$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t) - s(t-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=1.6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(1.6) & ≈\\frac{s(1.6)-s(1.6-0.2)}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(1.6)-s(1.4)}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.9-2.7}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.2}{0.2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at the time $t=1.6,$ the velocity is approximately $1\\, \\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092031 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "6e4ab84e-f722-557e-9740-25b3a0b3d660" :question/math-academy-id "e-6104" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/6e3f77e5b7d491e3ee43c8f8150065282d2abc259e3a88751b93ff15b27f492a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x) - g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=6.$ Applying the above formula with $x=6$ and $h=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{g(6)-g(6-4)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092032 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d864064-2833-530a-b421-a57f9ec5fce5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "fb3d12e8-e413-5300-8d3f-991ddfd2a893" :question/math-academy-id "e-6105" :question/problem "A factory produces fans. The total cost of producing $x$ fans, in dollars, is given by the function $C(x).$  The graph of $C(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/ad3fae3694c4df8269be9a54350aabf83368b3152ea69eb2b9d1ac76e702823f.png)\n\nApproximate the marginal cost $C'(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nC'(x) \\approx \\dfrac{C(x) - C(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=3.$ Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{C(3)-C(3-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{C(3)-C(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-20}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the marginal cost is $\\[math]5$ per fan."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494ef4f7-873c-5d87-85e5-89094c6cc376" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d29b44f2-49df-5106-8c47-3ed86fa2e77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336155} {:answer/id #uuid "0a14edba-101f-5f0f-8493-29f65b5ad90d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a14edba-101f-5f0f-8493-29f65b5ad90d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336156} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d17bca4-dfa8-5637-80e2-f68f77f47757" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca6e7459-ab08-5f57-bb26-c78c226031a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343779" :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $0.7$ | $0.8$ | $0.9$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $13$ | $15$ | $18$ | $20$ | $22$ | $25$ |\n\nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=1.2$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\,\\,{f}^{′}(1.2)≈$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=1.2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1.2) & ≈\\frac{f(1.2)-f(1.2-0.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1.2)-f(1.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-22}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{0.1} \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494ef4f7-873c-5d87-85e5-89094c6cc376" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0893f434-ab7d-50b6-8c21-5cba86c52d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0893f434-ab7d-50b6-8c21-5cba86c52d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336159} :answer-field/id #uuid "5de84170-0bd4-5655-95ef-0e75e8996856" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "464a1358-3b78-5c7e-9875-a51dcad5a536" :question/math-academy-id "q-343782" :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.25$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $10$ | $9$ | $8$ | $6$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=0.75$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\,\\,{f}^{′}(0.75)≈$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=0.75.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=0.25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0.75) & ≈\\frac{f(0.75)-f(0.75-0.25)}{0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(0.75)-f(0.5)}{0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-5}{0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{0.25} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494ef4f7-873c-5d87-85e5-89094c6cc376" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f532fdad-6d44-567e-8e55-a73ddffeee75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336162} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0c0a7d-2feb-5df7-9804-99f3b8902915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b0c0a7d-2feb-5df7-9804-99f3b8902915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336163} :answer-field/id #uuid "f016f9dc-39b2-573f-b597-bceb05037c3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12b7a3fa-89bd-53d6-a916-5e09ca0e4446" :question/math-academy-id "q-343788" :question/problem "A distance function $s(t)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.02$ | $0.04$ | $0.06$ | $0.08$ | $0.10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ | $9$ | $13$ | $18$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity ${s}^{′}(t)$ at the point $t=0.06$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\,\\,{s}^{′}(0.06)≈$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t)-s(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=0.06.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=0.02.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(0.06) & ≈\\frac{s(0.06)-s(0.06-0.02)}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(0.06)-s(0.04)}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-5}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0.02} \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf9cf71c-9be8-5841-8514-5c46185ff838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf9cf71c-9be8-5841-8514-5c46185ff838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336166} :answer-field/id #uuid "29197f99-98d5-5393-abcb-5094b0a3fefa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aedcaddd-0808-5967-84ab-caaf86d93147" :question/math-academy-id "q-343816" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/2c633342d5dc1ddb1244d492d81b6b9c00ffe33858398a72acb2a73467d93bb0.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the approximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=2$ and $x=4.$*\n\n${g}^{′}(4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x)-g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=4.$ Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4)-g(4-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(4)-g(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a91b1534-01ba-57e1-a8db-7e5965f8b0f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a91b1534-01ba-57e1-a8db-7e5965f8b0f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336169} :answer-field/id #uuid "104a67c9-2c2b-5ec3-98ec-0cc137041014" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6b9060a-5e21-57bc-83ba-2a9d11c84c25" :question/math-academy-id "q-343833" :question/problem "The number of deer in a wildlife reserve, measured in hundreds, is given by the function $D(t)$, where $t$ is the time, in years, since the study began. The values of $D(t)$ for several values of $t$ are shown in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $D(t)$ | $5$ | $7$ | $10$ | $14$ | $19$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of the deer population when $t=4$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $t=4$ is\n{{field-1}} hundreds per year." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{D}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{D(t)-D(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=4.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{D(4)-D(4-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{D(4)-D(3)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{19-14}{1} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $t=4$ is $5$ hundreds per year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e03e049-5f3e-5ff7-9758-94d9b6aacec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e03e049-5f3e-5ff7-9758-94d9b6aacec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336172} :answer-field/id #uuid "bda948df-57d9-507a-bbc7-6c1f9ecc1325" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab708991-bfbd-575f-9834-64b4bea609d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343841" :question/problem "The battery charge of a delivery drone, measured in percent, is given by the function $B(t)$, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the drone was fully charged. The values of $B(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B(t)$ | $80$ | $72$ | $55$ | $40$ | $35$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of the battery charge when $t=6$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $t=6$ is\n{{field-1}} percent per hour." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{B}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{B(t)-B(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{B(6)-B(6-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{B(6)-B(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-55}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $t=6$ is $-\\frac{15}{2}$ percent per hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d864064-2833-530a-b421-a57f9ec5fce5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad5b6392-64ad-55fe-a988-3954b922cc3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336175} {:answer/id #uuid "2b043044-28cc-56a1-aa5c-5e5fbf2fb860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad5b6392-64ad-55fe-a988-3954b922cc3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336175} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc96f7f1-9038-5677-9efd-59393fa9de91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f719021c-ba29-5df8-bf19-5b8055797dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-343858" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/3a4526a6979a86aa924bddca54b05778e91bb67a1dcb6159c5436cc975b22668.png)\n\nA cyclist records the total distance traveled after $x$ minutes. The total distance traveled after $x$ minutes, in miles, is given by the function $D(x).$ The graph of $D(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=45$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=15.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=30$ and $x=45.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=45$ is\n{{field-1}} miles per minute." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{D}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{D(x)-D(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=45.$  Applying the above formula with $x=45$ and $h=15$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(45) & ≈\\frac{D(45)-D(45-15)}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{D(45)-D(30)}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{11-6}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=45$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$ miles per minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d864064-2833-530a-b421-a57f9ec5fce5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df9ac778-ac39-513d-95b0-966ef7c0c963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336179} {:answer/id #uuid "62094e1b-52dd-594e-aa6a-251ca1d942e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62094e1b-52dd-594e-aa6a-251ca1d942e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336180} :answer-field/id #uuid "8304bd21-4ab9-51f9-bcd9-dee51fb9070a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0b47029-aa71-51b9-a42b-48c9fb06a339" :question/math-academy-id "q-343862" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/9a5fa5cdd4734813d18af5b99e3869051f15e137f72e98ad5529c751e6f85d87.png)\n\nA model rocket’s height is recorded during a launch. The height after $x$ seconds, in meters, is given by the function $H(x).$ The graph of $H(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=12.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=4$ and $x=16.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=16$ is\n{{field-1}} meters per second." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{H}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{H(x)-H(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=16.$  Applying the above formula with $x=16$ and $h=12$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{′}(16) & ≈\\frac{H(16)-H(16-12)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{H(16)-H(4)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=16$ is $\\frac{1}{6}$ meters per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d864064-2833-530a-b421-a57f9ec5fce5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa295e88-7dd7-548a-8b7f-d90bc23f98b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa295e88-7dd7-548a-8b7f-d90bc23f98b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336183} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9ba6acf-8fbb-5bb3-8957-0d010726cc04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "771ddffa-f1c8-5e73-8081-54fdf5cab8e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343869" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/fd917228308b839be77110b07e32fa0154350c290920628400bd1f20a34160b5.png)\n\nA factory tracks the number of defective items remaining after a recall. The number of defective items after $x$ days, in hundreds, is given by the function $N(x).$ The graph of $N(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=15$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=10$ and $x=15.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=15$ is\n{{field-1}} hundred items per day." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{N(x)-N(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=15.$  Applying the above formula with $x=15$ and $h=5$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{′}(15) & ≈\\frac{N(15)-N(15-5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{N(15)-N(10)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{72-76}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=15$ is $-\\frac{4}{5}$ hundred items per day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d864064-2833-530a-b421-a57f9ec5fce5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9a6c514-0b33-583a-a334-181957f98242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336186} {:answer/id #uuid "f35d6dfd-1f7f-5da5-9f12-f9d159915369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336187} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb73932-a3f6-5f04-840f-519101f3fb93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{liter}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336188} {:answer/id #uuid "394e5480-69da-5762-987b-e2fa2fa71b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336189} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9ffc89-40d3-5fa3-8283-cf4e49db918a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "394e5480-69da-5762-987b-e2fa2fa71b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336189} :answer-field/id #uuid "23a15028-c5cb-55d5-88d6-5f1818ed5cbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "356c9356-b943-5cfb-849c-968981b6e053" :question/math-academy-id "q-47357" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/64e35c559b233a28c5913ffa37ad949653ad2b46a0e10db639626b8575b7a263.png)\n\nAn oil tank is leaking $q(t)$ liters of oil, where $t>0$ is the time in hours since the leak started.\n The graph of $q(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=2.$ Applying the above formula with $t=2$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{q(2)-q(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the rate of leaking is $2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c64f2e-b43a-56a1-90c3-e8efb28e9ebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336193} {:answer/id #uuid "d04b1cb5-e482-52d4-99a5-84068c6245b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.015\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336194} {:answer/id #uuid "126ab19d-1501-5873-ac93-0ea8ca4db113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.025\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336195} {:answer/id #uuid "06d4619d-186b-51b7-b887-34a132b4ee51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336196} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b43a3f-869c-5f52-9480-41cab83bdd71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5b43a3f-869c-5f52-9480-41cab83bdd71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336197} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b11ff88-8211-5080-ace3-99db4ef73418" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4fbb16c-9818-55d9-b0fe-3dcb50b4a1b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47485" :question/problem "A baseball is hit by a batter. The distance between the batter and the ball, measured in miles, is given by the function $s(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $0.015$ | $0.03$ | $0.045$ | $0.05$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity ${s}^{′}(t)$ when $t=1$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t)-s(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{s(1)-s(1-0.5)}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(1)-s(0.5)}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.03-0.015}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.015}{0.5} \\\\  & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is $0.03\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "940a45ae-257e-55d1-9aed-25203f24b5f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090528} {:answer/id #uuid "9b859008-fda0-554d-8dd1-58c153af7052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090529} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f89e86-4de8-515a-aa0f-46012ac01996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090530} {:answer/id #uuid "b0efbc79-d303-51c7-870d-8ed8191d60d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090531} {:answer/id #uuid "6c53b1ff-3489-573b-915b-c3fa66c089f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0efbc79-d303-51c7-870d-8ed8191d60d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090531} :answer-field/id #uuid "54834ceb-5b32-5689-886c-6e4ca61599e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06c09dec-29e6-5d07-b108-0ae7fffc8186" :question/math-academy-id "q-47547" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/5773f1515892d8eb2cb77c700eab48f7cf44b0f91e9e157960798574e23ca93d.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x)-g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=2.$ . Applying the above formula with $x=2$ and $h=1$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{g(2)-g(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(2)-g(1)}{1} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb3687c9-788a-5d47-b00e-a4fe9094dd48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336200} {:answer/id #uuid "9c506356-a53e-5bbd-af4f-1982ebb0541e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336201} {:answer/id #uuid "93b9acb3-33eb-5ffb-8410-09aa06a11ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336202} {:answer/id #uuid "f82bfe38-b97b-5e1b-9268-8792ebb2ccd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336203} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc5c6c0-622e-585e-a2b9-618ad3635eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93b9acb3-33eb-5ffb-8410-09aa06a11ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336202} :answer-field/id #uuid "321ecaca-c4e3-55bf-a7ee-0995395e7491" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c53a6f73-17a8-5bbe-bd90-6cc3c6a1de68" :question/math-academy-id "q-47552" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/34145d4a924027964096df2eae6e87d31832833528aef06203a1b1f43f73d2ac.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=2.$ Applying the above formula with $x=2$ and $h=4$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{f(2)-f(2-4)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2)-f(-2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "774ce742-db3b-5c08-9889-ce4e5b236c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336207} {:answer/id #uuid "6d55cd76-bc9d-5826-b06f-a28f74822c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336208} {:answer/id #uuid "c0945dfb-548f-5d3e-97be-6531861e4f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-31\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336209} {:answer/id #uuid "cd330cec-b2bb-5b88-b0ec-98992ae9c5fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336210} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4b15de-49cb-5838-92b6-c5fac12decb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d55cd76-bc9d-5826-b06f-a28f74822c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336208} :answer-field/id #uuid "24dca563-27e2-5ebb-9cf9-a88591dea56d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "890f170a-8fa1-5f2c-9033-51da6ad42226" :question/math-academy-id "q-51328" :question/problem "The altitude $h(t)$ of a falling object object, measured in meters, is recorded every second for an interval of six seconds. The values of $h(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $h(t)$ | $200$ | $195$ | $181$ | $159$ | $128$ | $91$ | $51$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of the altitude when $t=3$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{h(t)-h(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=3.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{h(3)-h(3-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{h(3)-h(2)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{159-181}{1} \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the rate of change of the altitude is $-22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494ef4f7-873c-5d87-85e5-89094c6cc376" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "828e1a03-939b-5bf9-bf61-46f9b4788ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336214} {:answer/id #uuid "e56f600e-4430-5dfc-8938-98f4bd31e89b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336215} {:answer/id #uuid "f6897621-308f-5690-af48-7fc5f4da0539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336216} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1be5b1-ce58-5627-82dd-484adbeab071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336217} {:answer/id #uuid "5116db57-81ae-5608-b168-f4d1f926008c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "828e1a03-939b-5bf9-bf61-46f9b4788ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336214} :answer-field/id #uuid "194760d2-f0d7-52fc-9ccc-9f0d387e1759" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3f4ee99-f12e-56c7-87fa-957b023622f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-72052" :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.9$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.9$ |\n\nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=-1$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=-1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ≈\\frac{f(-1)-f(-1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(-1)-f(-2)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1-0.4}{1} \\\\  & =-0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494ef4f7-873c-5d87-85e5-89094c6cc376" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123aefd0-158d-58c8-ab2d-327d2f43c0aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336221} {:answer/id #uuid "0259c0f5-87a5-51b7-a0c4-53d54f1dc218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336222} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc4b522-cb2c-5f17-ab68-68f1eb8af718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336223} {:answer/id #uuid "e550f798-2bf9-5a0a-b69e-8ee79b5d1ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336224} {:answer/id #uuid "88bf47ba-bbc3-5182-a2e1-380cb67d106b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "123aefd0-158d-58c8-ab2d-327d2f43c0aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336221} :answer-field/id #uuid "e887a8de-30ef-5192-a1b2-4ef6e194a3c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a970a57-1ac6-5681-a1e8-a38215fcd8f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72065" :question/problem "A function $g(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $217$ | $234$ | $268$ | $336$ | $478$ |\n\nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x)-g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{g(2)-g(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(2)-g(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{268-234}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{1} \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5f35c31-2929-56ff-9839-8e226f845f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336228} {:answer/id #uuid "ca07eec7-fcb8-50a6-aaa3-6c331f43d7a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336229} {:answer/id #uuid "942daf93-3b42-5c19-b08f-253740e4ee38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336230} {:answer/id #uuid "0612d428-53ce-5d4b-8ccb-8acb67fbd891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336231} {:answer/id #uuid "77bd4ad6-3b1b-59fe-b2b5-b14e3c15d24d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca07eec7-fcb8-50a6-aaa3-6c331f43d7a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336229} :answer-field/id #uuid "dea59eec-b575-5bd4-9915-771ed028c851" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2365a128-ce89-54a9-89a7-9cb8a874750f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72067" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/a93150906c0681b8654370267fb918076338fa8ab5bf68f50a0062a4ab34af1d.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=-1$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=-1.$ Applying the above formula with $x=-1$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ≈\\frac{f(-1)-f(-1-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(-1)-f(-3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-(-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b3a3dba-83f7-5aab-9d33-cb1bfa2f4602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336235} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5b7d39-867b-5456-bc82-2ef2915ad225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336236} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb7aa40-937f-5f17-8d4f-fb68e5c4cf71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336237} {:answer/id #uuid "382eb46f-d9f9-5a72-a728-4c356bdd4802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336238} {:answer/id #uuid "4c30de69-4592-553c-a70a-999ef262b75a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e5b7d39-867b-5456-bc82-2ef2915ad225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336236} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7995560-f7fe-5f57-bdb5-4b845a4b1015" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9717b0a-0867-5415-96c9-6481c5224384" :question/math-academy-id "q-72074" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/894d659f3ef4544eeda653f5e098d9ca26f3e06899798441534a1bf395628d0e.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=3.$ Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3)-f(3-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3)-f(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e941df27-1d0f-554a-8daf-8c62f00a0b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ liters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336242} {:answer/id #uuid "75023b24-2f49-5a3e-8bc6-053d983b628c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ liters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336243} {:answer/id #uuid "d779623d-6921-5907-a3a1-16f4f70dcf50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ liters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336244} {:answer/id #uuid "6da3c470-8ff4-526b-81e8-19b2d9dd2036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ liters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336245} {:answer/id #uuid "cd866a51-dfe4-56ca-8bfb-57866c3b9630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6$ liters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd866a51-dfe4-56ca-8bfb-57866c3b9630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6$ liters per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336246} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b8d5130-0515-5454-8e63-1391311683e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ae282ea-b8e3-5393-964f-989b3735926f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72075" :question/problem "An oil tank is leaking. The volume of oil in the tank, in liters, after $t$ hours is given by the function $q(t).$ The values of $q(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $q(t)$ | $200$ | $192$ | $186$ | $182$ | $180$ |\n\nApproximate the rate at which the volume of oil in the tank is changing when $t=2$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{q(2)-q(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =186-192 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the rate of change of the volume is $-6$ liters per hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d864064-2833-530a-b421-a57f9ec5fce5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e981eeab-d597-50b4-9af0-71828f72945c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336249} {:answer/id #uuid "b44aff20-12b3-5736-a688-3b1c2bd02343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336250} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2337f3-4b08-5d0e-b98d-65f3d5113587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336251} {:answer/id #uuid "96f0d52e-57ec-546c-a6ec-c03ec2a4430e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336252} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4b6dbc-2b9e-5e2f-bee4-437d00075fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "800\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b44aff20-12b3-5736-a688-3b1c2bd02343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336250} :answer-field/id #uuid "627ea0c3-c603-50d6-b534-98410e1b7c01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fee9c3a-f0bf-5a94-a09d-085f67b90a28" :question/math-academy-id "q-86484" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/1fcdf0d68d78cba445d1e3ecfeec237ee4e33d7c74cb3b292593fe8b717fe6b8.png)\n\nPeter notices that the total number of eggs produced on his farm $t$ weeks after the beginning of his observations is given by the function $N(t).$ The graph of $N(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the number of produced eggs increases when $t=5$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{N(t)-N(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=5.$ Applying the above formula with $t=5$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{′}(5) & ≈\\frac{N(5)-N(5-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{N(5)-N(4)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{800-600}{1} \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the rate of increase is $200\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}.$"}]}
